      "## Question and scope\n\nThe study measures the record and the handoff, not the professional judgment of engineering manager or the legal outcome of the work. State how duplicate requests are identified and whether they are merged, linked, or counted separately. Define the start event, the stop event, the pause rule, and the treatment of a missing ticket before collecting data. Keep the protocol with the ticket intake batch so the next reviewer can see what was measured. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification.\n\nThe protocol does not estimate an industry benchmark; it produces a local description that engineering managers can review. A count without a denominator is not a measurement, especially when the queue changes size during the day. Write the primary measure and one supporting measure; a measure without a denominator or observation window is not ready. Separate the operational question from the compliance question; this method does not certify either outcome. Keep the evidence reference beside the coded value so a reviewer can test the same case without rebuilding the queue.",
      "## Population and unit\n\nTest the unit definition on a case that crosses owners, a case that is still open, and a case that was reopened. A case that moves between owners stays one case unless the process defines a new requirement at the handoff. The start event should be the first recorded request, not the first time the team opened the case. If the queue was archived during the window, report the loss and exclude the affected period rather than assuming the cases were resolved. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it.\n\nTest the unit definition on a case that crosses owners, a case that is still open, and a case that was reopened. Record the queue at the start and end of each observation day so additions and withdrawals are visible. A correction should be classified by the original case and by the correction event, with both dates retained. A case that never received an owner still belongs in the population; do not drop it because it is difficult to classify. At Outsourced Programmers, the reviewer should be able to explain this step to product owner from the case record alone.",
      "## Definitions and measures\n\nA case that has no destination change should be defined as cancelled or rejected, not counted as complete. Keep the original text and the coded value together so a reviewer can check the classification. A duplicate request is a separate intake event even when it points to the same underlying need; record the link. If two reviewers disagree, preserve both classifications until the definition is clarified. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document.\n\nA case that has no destination change should be defined as cancelled or rejected, not counted as complete. Define allowed values for status, reason, owner, and evidence state before collection. Before collection, test the definitions on a missing deployment window, a reopened case, and a handoff. A definition that depends on the reviewer\u2019s memory is not reproducible enough for this study. When a case needs an exception, record the reason, the owner, and the date before continuing the review.",
      "## Data dictionary and evidence\n\nKeep free-text notes separate from coded fields so the analysis does not depend on inconsistent wording. Keep the identity crosswalk in the approved system and restrict it to the review owner. A missing value should have a reason code, not a blank cell or a zero. Use a separate field for reviewer notes so the coded values stay comparable. Keep the measurement note short enough to read with the case and specific enough to support the reported review turnaround.\n\nIf the source is a document, record the page or field; if the source is a system, record the record identifier and extraction time. Give every case a study key that is not the client name, member number, or candidate email. Record the source identifier, the field name, the observation time, and the reviewer for every extracted value. Pilot the dictionary with two reviewers and compare their classifications before the main sample. If two reviewers disagree, preserve both classifications until the definition or the evidence is resolved.\n\nIf the source is a document, record the page or field; if the source is a system, record the record identifier and extraction time. Version the dictionary and report which version was used for each observation period. Keep the original evidence reference next to the coded value so the reviewer can verify the classification. Use a separate field for reviewer notes so the coded values stay comparable. Link the observation to the exact field in the incident channel and to the QA evidence that supports it.",
      "## Sampling and collection\n\nIf the population is small, report every case and the reason the sample is exhaustive rather than presenting a false precision. A second reviewer should re-extract a small subsample and record disagreements before the full analysis. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. Record the sampling frame as an export with a date, filter, and row count. Do not let a summary written later replace the evidence that was available at the first decision.\n\nInclude adverse cases: a missing QA evidence, a conflicting source, an unavailable developer, and a tight deadline. Do not clean the data by filling missing values; preserve the missing state and report it as a result. Keep the sample selection reproducible by recording the export date, filter, and random seed or start rule. Record the date of the sample and the version of the queue export. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time.\n\nInclude adverse cases: a missing ticket, a conflicting source, an unavailable QA lead, and a tight deadline. Collect the case from the issue tracker, the ticket, and the approval record, and stop at the verified close. Include reopened, unresolved, and exception cases in the sample frame. Use a random start and a fixed interval when the frame is ordered, and record both. Keep the review note with the case so the next sampler does not have to reconstruct the decision from memory.",
      "## Analysis and reporting\n\nSeparate observation from interpretation; a high completion rate on a small easy sample is not evidence of a strong control. A small sample should be reported with the number of cases that drive the result. Keep the case-level table available for review and summarize only the measures that answer the question. End with the decision the evidence supports and the decision it does not support. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract.\n\nReport the eligible count, the sample, the missing-field rate, the exception rate, and the unresolved count at the close of the window. The analysis should end with the decision the evidence can support and the decision it cannot support. Use a simple before-and-after comparison only when the definitions and observation window are the same. Keep descriptive findings separate from causal claims and label exploratory results. At Outsourced Programmers, the ticket intake review should end with a dated note and a named owner for the next check.",
      "## Limitations\n\nSmall samples are sensitive to one case; report the instability instead of presenting a percentage as a stable rate. The study cannot determine whether a professional judgment was correct, only whether the record supports it. Do not treat a missing record as proof that the work did not happen; it is evidence about the record. If the queue changed during the window, explain how the change affects the denominator. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content.\n\nChanges in definitions, staffing, or the code repository during the window can create a trend that is not a process effect. The study cannot determine whether a professional judgment was correct, only whether the record supports it. State that the result describes the observed sample and not the entire industry or every team in the service. If the reviewer performed the work, use a second reviewer for a subsample and report the disagreement. The note should distinguish what was observed, what was inferred, and what still needs an owner decision.",
      "## Review and next action\n\nChoose one next action: fix the source, simplify the pull request, change the queue, adjust access, or pause the work until the owner decides. If the action changes failed deployment, record the approval from QA lead before implementation. Record the owner, the due date, and the exact change that will be tested at the next review. Report a failed action with the same detail as a successful one so the team can learn from it. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification.\n\nChoose one next action: fix the source, simplify the code review, change the queue, adjust access, or pause the work until the owner decides. Keep the review note with the ticket intake evidence so the next owner can see why the process changed. Set the next sample date before closing the review, so the action does not drift. Keep the prior procedure available so the next reviewer can compare the old and new states. Keep the evidence reference beside the coded value so a reviewer can test the same case without rebuilding the queue."
